WEBVTT

00:00.550 --> 00:15.076
Welcome. In this demonstration, we will build an automatic parking barrier
using an Arduino Uno, an ultrasonic sensor, and a small servo motor. Pause at
any time when you need more time.

00:16.550 --> 00:33.332
The robot follows three stages. First, the ultrasonic sensor detects a nearby
vehicle. Next, the Arduino compares the distance with fifteen centimetres.
Finally, the servo raises or lowers the barrier.

00:34.850 --> 00:51.248
Before starting, gather every item shown here. You need the Arduino,
ultrasonic sensor, servo, breadboard, jumper wires, USB cable, cardboard,
tape, scissors, a toy car, and a computer with Arduino IDE.

00:52.750 --> 01:08.020
Safety comes first. Keep the USB cable disconnected while wiring. Use only
five volts for the servo. Test the servo before adding cardboard, and keep
liquids away from the circuit.

01:09.550 --> 01:24.148
Step one. Connect Arduino five volts to the red positive breadboard rail. Then
connect an Arduino ground pin to the blue or black negative rail. Keep the USB
cable unplugged.

01:25.650 --> 01:39.648
Step two. Add the ultrasonic sensor. Connect its V C C pin to the positive
rail, and its ground pin to the negative rail. Leave trigger and echo
unconnected for now.

01:41.150 --> 01:57.212
Step three. Add the signal wires. Connect Arduino digital pin nine to the
sensor trigger pin. Connect Arduino digital pin ten to the sensor echo pin.
The sensor now has four connections.

01:58.750 --> 02:15.988
Step four. Connect the servo carefully. Red goes to the positive rail. Brown,
or black, goes to the negative rail. Orange, or yellow, is the signal wire and
connects to Arduino digital pin six.

02:17.450 --> 02:34.016
Step five. Read every connection aloud. Confirm that five volts never connects
to ground. Confirm all sensor and servo wires. When the check is complete,
connect USB and upload the program.

02:35.550 --> 02:50.724
Step six. Test the servo without cardboard first. Then disconnect USB, attach
a light cardboard strip to the white servo horn, reconnect USB, and test using
a toy car or your hand.

02:52.250 --> 03:05.312
Use this complete diagram to check every pin. The realistic photographs help
you recognise the parts, but the printed pin names in this diagram are the
authority for electrical connections.

03:06.850 --> 03:22.096
Open Arduino IDE, select Arduino Uno, and choose the correct port. Paste the
supplied program and click Upload. The program opens the barrier when an
object is fifteen centimetres away or closer.

03:23.550 --> 03:37.716
Before adding cardboard, open Serial Monitor at nine thousand six hundred
baud. Move your hand close to the sensor and check that the servo turns. Move
your hand away and check that it returns.

03:39.250 --> 03:53.032
If the circuit does not work, check one issue at a time. Verify power and
ground, then the sensor pins, then the three servo wires. Remove a heavy
barrier before testing again.

03:54.550 --> 04:09.316
To demonstrate understanding, identify the sensor, controller, actuator, and
power source. Change the detection distance from fifteen to twenty-five
centimetres, upload again, and explain the result.

04:10.850 --> 04:22.904
Your automatic parking barrier is ready. Move the toy car toward the sensor,
observe the gate opening, and explain the input, process, and output to your
class.
